00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.250 align:start position:0%
 
all right let's get to audience  questions for the week there are some  good ones hey David can you talk a  little bit about how Democrats can  appeal to rural and farm voters so this  this is a great question because it it  is connected to a lot of issues that in  2016 ultimately swung the election from  hillary clinton to donald trump  electoral ii if not in the popular vote  it's important to understand the context  here in the United States where the  geography already favors Republicans  there's an urban rural divide where when  we've interviewed professors who are  experts in in districting they've  explained that even if you account for  gerrymandering or if you fixed partisan  gerrymandering the way that the  electoral vote system works in  combination with the fact that there is  an urban rural divided urban voters  tending to be more on the Left rural  voters tending to be more on the right  you would still have a systemic  advantage under the electoral vote  system for Republicans even if you fixed  of overtly partisan gerrymandering and  that's why we have to have a more  nuanced analysis of this there's no  question that Donald Trump's policies  particularly trade policies have been  hugely damaging to farmers and yet  farmers still widely support Donald  Trump some are abandoning him we'll see  if any actually vote in a different way  in 2020 but by and large it's a  community that still supports Donald  Trump and what that tells us what we  have to understand is that the  urban-rural divide and appealing to  rural and farm voters by democrats is  bigger than just a list of policies like  if you just go talk to these  conservative farmers and say here's how  I would fix the Trump trade policy and  here's how it would be good for you  they're not just going to say oh that's  a good point I'll vote for a Democrat  instead of a Republican this time around  this is a cultural divide that exists in  many ways in one sense social issues  like abortion LGBT stuff race black live  matters and Tifa the war on Christmas in  one sense that stuff matters a lot to  some of these rural voters and they want  to feel like they have someone on their  side but what I think is important to  understand is that while there's a big  cultural component here and there is a  group like for example evangelical  Christians where it's just what  your position on abortion that is all  that matters and Trump claimed the  correct position as far as they're  concerned even if it's transparently  unbelievable and he ultimately got the  evangelical Christian vote the the  important thing I think for the left  understand is that they need to see us  as at least putative ly on their side to  some degree but it doesn't have to be  100% it just needs to be someone who's  not going to sort of flaunt the most  particular or acute cultural  disagreements right in their face so if  you can display as a Democratic  candidate a general sensitivity to the  values of rural voters but you're on the  same page economically that may pull in  a lot of these rural and farm voters now  you're not going to bring everybody over  or nearly everyone but these are  elections that by and large are won  around the margins Hillary Clinton  ultimately lost because of 77,000 votes  in three states so if you hear me saying  if you don't insult or rural voters with  social stuff and you are sort of on the  same page economically they'll vote for  you well know most of them still won't  but the point is we don't need them all  to vote for us we just need some to say  even if we're not exactly on the same  page socially and culturally I respect  the economic policy and my situation is  such that that's what I need to focus on  in choosing who I vote for this time  around there's probably a way even to  appeal to environmentalism that would  appeal more to a rural voter maybe  related to hunting and fishing and  resources for farming I'm thinking out  loud here can we deal with healthcare in  a way that will relate to the rural  experience few providers in many cases  difficult for people without ready  transportation access to physically get  to where the care is because it's often  very far gun control is definitely a  question mark I don't know how the left  that cares about gun safety deals with  guns for the rural community in a way  that will will resonate but maybe you  just stay away from that issue to some  of the grease but thinking strategically  an Elizabeth Warren in purse  is really good at this stuff it's  unclear whether you know it remains to  be seen whether at the national level in  debates it would work just as  effectively I'm not saying it would or  wouldn't but she is very good at meeting  with Midwestern and red state voters the  fact that she's from think Oklahoma  lived in Texas for a while probably  doesn't hurt and she is able to relate  to these rural voters on the issues that  might actually influence their voting  decision without insulting what are  obviously cultural and social  differences and and it would be great to  see other candidates do as good of a job  at it I've not seen any of the top-tier  Democratic candidates do as good of a  job at it as I've seen Elizabeth Warren  do

